    "created_at": "2016-08-01T06:42:20Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-08-01T06:42:20Z",
    "body": "It's a bit interesting that `value` resets the counter in the forward direction, even if the counters are `reversed`. So\n\n``` html\n<ol reversed>\n  <li>x</li>\n  <li value = 2>x</li>\n  <li>x</li>\n  <li>x</li>\n</ol>\n```\n\ngives  `4,2,1,0`; not `3,2,2,1`. This means that user-agents have to execute two traversals -- one to figure out the starting element, and one to actually number them. A single reverse-traversal isn't enough; unlike the single forward traversal that suffices for numbering things in the forward direction.\n\n(As always, my primary reaction to such edge cases is \"why would anyone _do_ such a thing\" 😝 )\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
